   "subject": "raster data for mask - errors",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi All,<br>I&#39;m running into issues using raster data for my habitat mask. Specifically, I have floating point GRID data file (reads as formal class raster layer in R) that I want to add as a covariate to my habitat mask. Based on secr documentation, &quot;The procedure depends on the data source spatialdata, which may be either a spatial coverage (raster or polygon) or an object with covariate values at points (another mask or traps<br>object).&quot; So raster data should be OK. These data load just fine into R but an error occurs when I try to addCovariate to the mask. <br><br><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:10pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:15px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">ndvi_14&lt;-raster(&quot;ndvi14_83cs&quot;)\r\n</span><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">rubmask2013male &lt;- addCovariates(rubmask2014male,ndvi_14,&quot;VALUE&quot;)\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Error in addCovariates(rubmask2014male, ndvi_14, &quot;VALUE&quot;) : \r\n  spatialdata type unrecognised or unsupported</span></pre><br>As a last resort, I can convert the raster to polygon but was wondering if anyone had a solution here. I converted my raster data from floating point to integer and secr still doesn&#39;t like recognize or support this data type. <br><br>Thanks for any comments/suggestions!<br>Annie<br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "FJ-NmZoiAwAJ",
   "from": "Murray Efford",
   "author": "118cea91a1619106",
   "date": "2016-11-03T23:58:21Z",
   "subject": "Re: raster data for mask - errors",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Annie<br><br>I think you are taking &quot;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\">raster or polygon&quot; too literally - raster is a general descriptor, </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"> &#39;RasterLayer&#39; is not mentioned as an input option, and the error message is explicit</span>. The two raster types supported are &#39;mask&#39; (from secr) and &#39;SpatialGridDataFrame&#39; (from sp). Note also the warning in ?addCovariates that </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\">input from a SpatialGridDataFrame is untested (I just haven&#39;t used that format much, but don&#39;t anticipate any problem).<br><br></span>It should be easy to coerce a RasterLayer to </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\">SpatialGridDataFrame something like this<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:10pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:15px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:blue\"><span style=\"color:#000\">ndvi_14_SGDF </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:blue\"><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ndvi_14</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;SpatialGridDataFrame&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span></pre></div></code></div></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"><br>Then you can use ndvi_14_SGDF in addCovariates. Let us know if this doesn&#39;t work<code>.<br></code></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"><code></code></span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\"></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;display:inline!important;float:none\">Murray<br><br><br></span></div>"
    ],
